active_hypotheses.Grok-4.6-H11.1:
Duhamel as in Dir.\ 7: \(A(z)=L[\delta_0]\oplus K\ast X\) in \(\mathbb F_2[[z]]\), with \(X=\sum\chi_{s,i}z^s\) the generating function of \(\chi=u\cdot\sigma_x u\) and \(K\) the Lucas/Sierpiński kernel (composition law known). If \(A\) were rational, deconvolution against lacunary \(K\) (support = 1-bits of \(n\)) would force \(X\) to have linear Newton polygon (degree \(\le C\cdot\mathrm{per}(A)\)). But \(\chi=u\cdot\sigma_x u\) is quadratic on a cone of width \(\Theta(t)\), so the Newton polygon of \(X\) is strictly superlinear unless \(\chi\) vanishes on a positive-density set of nested right-triangles. \emph{Conjecture:} these two Newton polygons cannot coincide for \(\mathrm{supp}\,u_0=\{0\}\).

Evades A/F: kernel support is the full cone. Evades C: the object is \(A\) itself. Evades H: the contradiction is a comparison of two entire polygons, not a finite Hankel rank. Evades D: Lucas is the composition law.

\textit{Rule-90 screen:} \(X\equiv0\), polygons are \(-\infty\), no clash.

